    "body": "The U.S. Coast Guard announced the purchase of four MQ-9B long-range unmanned aircraft systems, ground control stations and supporting equipment through a U.S. Customs and Border Protection contract. (Coast Guard)\nWASHINGTON — The Coast Guard is purchasing MQ-9B drones for the first time, ordering four of the high-flying unmanned aerial vehicles for just under $250 million.\nThe aircraft will support the service to oversee offshore waters, conduct search and rescue operations, and help counter drug trafficking operations, among other things, the Coast Guard said in a news release today. They are not expected to carry weapons, a service spokesperson told Breaking Defense.\n“The MQ-9B long-range unmanned aircraft systems will expand our reach and strengthen our ability to protect the nation,” Anthony Antognoli, the assistant commandant and program executive officer for robotics and autonomous systems for the Coast Guard, said in a statement. “This investment will give Coast Guard crews a longer-range view of our maritime domain, helping them detect threats earlier, make better-informed decisions and act faster across our missions.”\nThe Coast Guard first revealed its plans to purchase the drones last year, thanks to funding the service received from President Donald Trump’s “One Big Beautiful Bill” that passed in July 2025, Military Times previously reported .\nThe Coast Guard said today that funding for the MQ-9B acquisition stemmed from the FY25 reconciliation package that included nearly $25 billion for the service, along with FY26 appropriations. The MQ-9B purchase totaled $247.8 million for the four aircraft, along with ground control stations and other supporting equipment, the Coast Guard said.\nThe Coast Guard has previously teamed up with the US Customs and Border Protection (CBP) and flown CBP’s MQ-9A drones for operations out of Texas, according to a Coast Guard factsheet . The Coast Guard noted in today’s release that the aircraft supported CBP to seize 60,700 pounds of illegal drugs and the apprehension of roughly 20 “smugglers” in FY26.\nThe MQ-9B SeaGuardian is designed to conduct maritime intelligence, surveillance, and reconnaissance operations for more than 30 hours at a time with a mission radius of roughly 1,200 nautical miles, according to General Atomics , which builds the aircraft.\nBreaking Defense Video\nNew leaders, new drone command: What we expect from AUSA 2026\nWatch Now »\nUnpacking Boeing’s F/A-XX win, plus a report from wartime Ukraine\nWatch Now »\nHow Hegseth envisions a new Autonomous Warfare Command\nWatch Now »\nWhat Europe’s military air fleets will look like in 30 years\nWatch Now »\nRiyadh gets closer to F-35s, Damascus presses sovereignty and an expert take on Iran\nWatch Now »\nDrone-hunting on the border, plus turmoil at an Air Force think tank\nWatch Now »\nThe future of European airpower might be unmanned\nWatch Now »\nThe big news out of AFA, plus a guided tour of the show floor\nWatch Now »\nWhy the ‘right to repair’ matters to the Pentagon\nWatch Now »\nWhat the Pentagon is testing along the US-Mexico border\nWatch Now »\nScroll for more video",
